\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\lang1053\f0\fs18  \b WHY? \b0\line An under-defined sketch (blue entities) can shift unpredictably when upstream dimensions change. This silently corrupts downstream features, assemblies, and drawings without any rebuild error. In a production environment, a single floating line can cascade into a completely wrong part geometry before anyone notices. \line\line\b HOW? \b0\line\bullet Apply geometric relations first (Horizontal, Vertical, Tangent, Coincident) before adding dimensions. \line\bullet Confirm all entities turn \b black \b0 before exiting the sketch. Blue = under-defined, Black = fully defined, Red = over-defined. \line\bullet Use \b Sketch -> Check Sketch For Feature \b0 to detect problematic contours. \line\bullet If an entity cannot be constrained by geometry alone, use a driving dimension or a Fix relation as a last resort. \line\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Segoe UI;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\lang1053\f0\fs18  \b WHY? \b0\line Zero-draft or negative-draft faces prevent the part from being ejected from the mold without tearing or tool damage. This is discovered too late if not checked during design, leading to costly tool rework or scrapped parts. Every injection-molded and die-cast part must pass draft analysis before releasing the model to the toolmaker. \line\line\b HOW? \b0\line\bullet Run \b Evaluate -> Draft Analysis \b0 , set the pull direction to the parting line axis. \line\bullet Green faces = positive draft (safe). Red faces = negative draft (blocked). Yellow faces = zero draft (check with toolmaker). \line\bullet Minimum requirement: 1.5 degrees for smooth surfaces, 3 degrees for textured surfaces. \line\bullet Add draft using \b Insert -> Features -> Draft \b0 , referencing the parting line as the neutral plane. \line\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Segoe UI;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\lang1053\f0\fs18  \b WHY? \b0\line Manually sketched circular cuts carry no semantic data. Hole Wizard features embed the hole type, standard (ISO/ANSI), thread callout, and tolerances directly into the model. This data flows automatically into the Drawing Hole Table and into downstream ERP/MES systems. A manually cut 'bolt hole' can have the wrong diameter and nobody will know until assembly. \line\line\b HOW? \b0\line\bullet Always use \b Insert -> Features -> Hole Wizard \b0 for tapped holes, clearance holes, counterbores, and countersinks. \line\bullet Select the correct standard (ISO 4762, DIN 912, etc.) matching the fastener being used. \line\bullet Place hole positions on the second tab using sketch relations - do not guess positions. \line\bullet On drawings, insert a \b Hole Table \b0 to automatically capture all Hole Wizard holes in the view. \line\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Segoe UI;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\lang1053\f0\fs24  \b WHY? \b0\line Custom properties are the bridge between CAD and the rest of the business: ERP, procurement, quality, and production. When a BOM is generated from the drawing, it pulls its data directly from these fields. A missing Description means a blank cell in the purchase order. A wrong Part Number means the wrong item gets ordered. This directly impacts production schedules and costs. \line\line\b HOW? \b0\line\bullet Open \b File -> Properties -> Custom \b0 in every part and assembly file before release. \line\bullet Mandatory fields: PartNumber, Description, Material, Revision, Vendor (if purchased). \line\bullet PartNumber must be unique across the entire product database. Verify in PDM before assigning. \line\bullet For standard purchased parts (fasteners, bearings), fill the Vendor and VendorPN fields so procurement can order directly from the BOM. \line\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Segoe UI;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\lang1053\f0\fs18  \b WHY? \b0\line Fillets and chamfers placed early in the feature tree create parent-child references that break every time upstream geometry is modified. Modifying a base extrusion width to fix a tolerance can instantly cause ten downstream fillets to fail, turning a simple edit into a long error-fixing session. Fillets should be the last things added and the first things suppressed when troubleshooting. \line\line\b HOW? \b0\line\bullet Complete all structural features (extrusions, cuts, holes, patterns, mirrors) before adding any fillets or chamfers. \line\bullet Create a dedicated folder at the bottom of the tree called 'FINISHING_FEATURES' and drag all fillets and chamfers into it. \line\bullet When debugging rebuild errors, suppress the entire folder first to isolate whether fillets are causing the problem. \line\bullet Functional radii (stress-relief fillets, sealing grooves) are exceptions - place them near the functional feature they belong to. \line\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Segoe UI;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\lang1053\f0\fs18  \b WHY? \b0\line Without a complete revision history, it is impossible to know what changed between Rev A and Rev C, who approved it, or why it was changed. This is a regulatory and quality management requirement in ISO 9001 environments. In the event of a field failure or warranty claim, traceability to the engineering change that caused the issue is legally and contractually required. \line\line\b HOW? \b0\line\bullet Insert a Revision Table via \b Insert -> Tables -> Revision Table \b0 in the drawing template. \line\bullet Each row must contain: Revision letter, Date, Description of change, Drawn by, Approved by, and ECN (Engineering Change Notice) number. \line\bullet Revision letters must match the PDM lifecycle state. Do not manually increment revision letters outside of the PDM workflow. \line\bullet Mark changed areas on the drawing with revision clouds and a balloon pointing to the revision table row. \line\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Segoe UI;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\lang1053\f0\fs18  \b WHY? \b0\line A modeled 3D helical thread on an M10 bolt contains hundreds of thousands of triangles. Multiply this by 50 bolts in an assembly and the graphics card must process millions of extra triangles that convey zero engineering information beyond what a cosmetic thread symbol already shows. Large assemblies with modeled threads become unresponsive and crash-prone. Cosmetic threads display correctly on drawings and carry all required callout data. \line\line\b HOW? \b0\line\bullet Never use \b Insert -> Features -> Thread \b0 (the 3D modeled thread) for standard fastener threads in production models. This feature is for visualization and CNC programming only. \line\bullet Use \b Insert -> Annotations -> Cosmetic Thread \b0 on cylindrical faces. Specify the thread standard, size, and depth. \line\bullet On drawings, cosmetic threads display as the standard dashed circle / line representation per ISO 6410 or ASME conventions automatically. \line\bullet For Toolbox fasteners, cosmetic threads are already applied. Verify the depth matches the engagement length in the mating tapped hole. \line\par
}

